ised*, then *opponent called* — so the pot is currently "checked to completion" only if both check. But actually, after you raised 100 and opponent called (0 means call when facing bet), the round ended with both having matched bets.\n\nSo now on the River, **it\'s a new round**, starting with you to act first. Since no one has bet yet, you can either **check (0)** or **bet** (min-bet is typically big blind level — not specified, but your smallest raise option listed is 100, suggesting min-bet is 100).\n\nTherefore:\n- **Check (0):** Lets opponent bluff or value-bet their made hands. If they have missed, they might bet air; if they have flush or better, they may bet for value. Then you can fold.\n- **Bet 100:** Small probe. Might get called by worse pairs (like 77, 66, A-high with weak kicker), but unlikely. Most hands worse than yours probably won’t call. Hands better than yours (flush, two pair, set) will likely call or raise.\n- **Fold (-1):** Too strong a reaction — you still have showdown value as pair of 8s, especially since you’ve been aggressive.\n- **All-in (-2):** Risky. You have ~19,650 chips. All-in would put immense pressure, but if opponent has flush, they’ll call and win. Only time it works is if they fold a marginal hand — but many will just call with weaker pairs or draws that improved.\n\nBest line: **Check (0)** to control pot size and avoid bloating it with a weak one pair. Let opponent show weakness or bluff.\n\nReason:  \nYou have a marginal hand on a dangerous board where flush is likely. Previous aggression doesn’t justify further investment without improvement. Checking keeps the pot manageable and allows you to respond to opponent’s action rationally. Betting invites calls from better hands and folds from worse ones — poor equity realization.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
