icating possible weakness or a passive strategy. However, this could also be a trap with strong hole cards.  \n- Since the opponent didn’t raise, I’m not facing aggression, so folding isn't necessary unless I believe my hand has no post-flop playability.  \n- Raising would be deceptive but risky with such a weak hand; it could win no value and might get called only by better hands.  \n- Checking (0) keeps me in the hand cheaply to see the flop, which may improve my hand (e.g., hitting a straight draw, trips, or pairing the 10).  \n- Going all-in (-2) is highly aggressive and unjustified given my poor starting hand — likely results in losing more chips when behind.  \n- A raise (e.g., 200) lacks justification here as I have little equity and no positional or image advantage specified.\n\nReason:  \nWith a marginal hand like 4♣ T♠, the optimal approach is to play low-risk and low-cost. Since the opponent checked, I can safely check back and see the flop without investing more chips. This preserves my chip lead and avoids committing resources with a hand that has poor showdown value.  \n\nAnswer: 0"}
